I just had a critcal realisation for the verified computing aspect of this problem. To maintain correctness, we do not need to sample until the confidence is 1, we need only sample until we can be sure that the original distribution and the submitted distribution are statistically indistinguishable. Thus, a task for this project will be computing the sample size needed. There is a possibility that we cannot reach a lower bound than the confidence bound, but it would be ideal to obtain a result that is in log log n. 

The confidence bound is in linear time if i recall correctly. Since the confidence represents a geometric variable with parameter norm(error),

#Calculate the parameter for the confidence bound on a geometric variable with unknown parameter e where e \in ##Calculate the range of potential parametres.

The key idea is that since the individual game values not what is important, as long as the distributions provided remain (perhaps even negligibly) close in statistial distance, our computations on those distributions will also be (perhaps negligibly) close, as opposed to the stronger indistinguishability result we might look for.

Should potentially look into PCP languages which can aggregate and perform the actions needed.

